INT. DEBTORS' PRISON WARD, LONDON - 1788 - NIGHT

A vaulted brick cell lit by a single tallow candle. Straw on the stones. A barred window, a crust of ice on the sill. Water drips somewhere in the walls.

Four prisoners share the space. EZEKIEL PRIMROSE-ADEBAYO (60s, once a draper, now a coat of patches) lies on a plank bed with a ledger on his chest. The young JEMIMA HARGREAVES-OKORO (20s, ragged dress, hair under a cap) sits by the candle, darning a stocking. The old soldier SERGEANT MAGNUS FALKENBERG (50s, one leg, a crutch) leans against the wall whittling a spoon. In the shadows, the child TOBIAH (8, barefoot, a ratty blanket over his shoulders) listens.

EZEKIEL
They say the King is mad again.

MAGNUS
They said so in 'eighty-eight.

EZEKIEL
They said so in 'eighty-seven.

MAGNUS
He'll be mad till he's dead.

JEMIMA
Hush. The boy.

TOBIAH
I'm not asleep.

JEMIMA
You should be.

TOBIAH
It's too cold.

Magnus sets down the spoon, shuffles on his crutch to the boy, and wraps the blanket tighter, tucking it under the child's heels. Ezekiel closes the ledger and sits up, grunting. Jemima bites off her thread and holds the stocking out to Tobiah, who pulls it over a bare foot. For a moment all four are close in the candlelight, the soldier kneeling, the draper leaning, the girl smiling at the child, and the child staring back at all of them as if memorizing their faces.

MAGNUS
Tomorrow it will be warmer.

TOBIAH
Is that a promise?

MAGNUS
It's a weather report.

TOBIAH
From who?

MAGNUS
From the Almighty. He writes to me.

Tobiah giggles. Jemima shakes her head. Ezekiel lies back down.

EZEKIEL
Then ask Him about the bailiff.
